<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  FastDDS unicast peer discovery — workstation <-> bernard-2.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  Measured 2026-08-08 on the lab WiFi: multicast to 225.0.0.1 (the group
  `ros2 multicast send/receive` uses) crosses between the workstation and the
  robot fine, but multicast to 239.255.0.1 — the group FastDDS actually uses
  for SPDP discovery — is silently dropped by the access point. Every other
  check passes: same subnet, no firewall, unicast works both ways (ssh), both
  ends are rmw_fastrtps_cpp, ROS_DOMAIN_ID=28 on both. Nodes on the robot are
  simply never discovered from the workstation.

  So `ros2 multicast receive` succeeding is NOT evidence that DDS discovery
  will work. It tests a different group. Do not use it to rule out this fault.

  This file replaces multicast discovery with an explicit unicast peer list.
  Each participant sends its discovery announcements directly to the listed
  addresses. No discovery server, no extra process.

  NOTE: this is a different fix from the previous team's
  /root/fastdds_unicast.xml. Theirs disabled the shared-memory transport to
  get data across the robot's container boundary; it does not address
  discovery and contains no peer list. Both fixes can be needed at once.

  USE
    export FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/path/to/fastdds_unicast_peers.xml

  MAINTENANCE
  The addresses below are hardcoded. If either machine's DHCP lease moves,
  discovery breaks with no error message. Prefer static leases; if an address
  changes, update it here and nowhere else.
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <participant profile_name="unicast_peer_discovery" is_default_profile="true">
    <rtps>
      <builtin>
        <initialPeersList>
          <!-- bernard-2 (robot, wlP1p1s0) -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>192.168.1.154</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
          <!-- dev workstation (wlan0) -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>192.168.1.144</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
          <!-- Loopback, so nodes on the same host still find each other
               once multicast is no longer doing that job for us. -->
          <locator>
            <udpv4>
              <address>127.0.0.1</address>
            </udpv4>
          </locator>
        </initialPeersList>
      </builtin>
    </rtps>
  </participant>
</profiles>
